  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n- Central Idea: We implement a population-based optimization strategy using the Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES) algorithm. This method explores the solution space more effectively by maintaining a probabilistic distribution of individuals. The approach combines multi-resolution optimization with repair mechanisms to escape local optima.\n- Why this could improve the parent: CMA-ES handles non-convex problems well and has fewer hyperparameter adjustments than gradient-based methods. We also incorporate a trainable representation using radial basis functions to smoother landscape changes when modifying grid resolution.\n- Bottleneck Addressed: The parent solution likely gets stuck in local optima. This approach directly explores the hypersphere of solutions, maintaining feasibility through repair functions, and focuses on regions with promising low correlation values.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h(x) = \u03a3 w_i * rbf(x - \u03bc_i) \n- Variables: weights (w, shape n) and centers (\u03bc, shape k) where k \u2264 n/2\n- Objective: min max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \u2248 max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k] * (2/n)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222b h(x) dx = 1\n- Repair: Clip values [0,1] then project to satisfy integral constraint using adaptive scaling\n- Fitness: Negative log-correlation penalty function to steer towards solutions with lower correlation peaks\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n    - (a) Parent Validation: Verify initial solution meets integral and bounds\n    - (b) Representation: Vectorize centers and weights; h(x) = sum w_i * exp(-(x - \u03bc_i)^2 / (2\u03c3^2))\n    - (c) Objective: Compute np.correlate penalty for both directions, log-scaled\n    - (d) Initialization: Latin hypercube sampling for \u03bc and uniform [0,0.5] for w\n    - (e) Hyperparameters: \u03c30=0.5, mutation rate=0.1, repair calls=5/individual\n    - (f) Mating: L-coupling with active weights\n    - (g) Repair: Singular value projection for infeasibility\n    - (h) CMA-ES Integration: Use scipy.optimize.cma.minimize with custom feasibility callback\n\n4. Search Choices\n    - Population Size: 30-50 based on grid complexity\n    - \u03c3 Evolution: \u03bc-strategy with \u03b7=0.3\n    - Dimension: Fixed at nearest multiple of 32 to 280\n    - Objective Transformation: log(1 + corr) to better rank improvements\n    - Repair Probability: Only if random repair outcome better\n\n5. Budget & Execution\n    - Estimated cost: O(n*log(n)) with automatic \u03c3 tracking\n    - Grace checks: Solve penalty log(1+10) = 20.5 worse than best -> break\n    - Warm start: Repair+10% phase before main CMA-ES\n    - Fallback: Repair(UBO) on early termination\n\n6. Validation\n    - Repair checks: Integral=560, bounds=[0,1]\n    - Feasibility: Custom repair on any candidate\n    - Orange book: Multi-start repair(min, max)\n    - Wrapper: cma.minimize with best-of strategy\n    - Destroy/Reconstruct: Repair(child) on obstacles\n    - Time sync: Budget division by \u03a3 log(params) + repair",
